input.content:
{"set": {
 "evidenceSummary": "Thirteen untrained men (mean age 21.85 years) completed a double-blind, placebo-controlled crossover trial comparing 150 mg per day CBD oil with placebo oil, separated by a 2-week washout, after 6 sets of 10 maximal eccentric elbow-flexor contractions. Condition-by-time interactions and condition main effects were not significant for perceived soreness, arm circumference, hanging joint angle, peak torque or RPE (P > 0.05). Collapsed across conditions, soreness, joint angle and RPE changed over time, while arm circumference and peak torque showed no significant time effect.",
 "screeningLimitations": "The sample was small and untrained, with one dose (about 2 mg/kg per day) and only noninvasive markers, so the trial cannot rule out effects in trained athletes, at other doses or on blood markers of damage. The damage stimulus was modest (a 12% peak torque drop that was not significant over time, versus 21% to 44% in earlier studies), which limits the chance of detecting a recovery benefit.",
 "paper.tldr": "A 150 mg daily CBD dose taken after eccentric arm exercise did not change soreness, arm swelling, joint angle or strength compared with placebo in untrained men. Soreness and joint angle changed over time in both conditions, showing the exercise caused some muscle damage.",
 "paper.practicalImplications": "For untrained adults, this dose and schedule of CBD oil would not be expected to reduce soreness or range-of-motion loss after heavy eccentric work, so the evidence does not support using it as a recovery aid at this dose."
},
"verification": {
 "status": "CORRECTED",
 "changes": [
  "evidenceSummary: said joint angle and RPE changed 'in both conditions' and omitted RPE from the null list -> time effects stated as collapsed across conditions, RPE added to the null condition effects, and no time effect for arm circumference or peak torque noted ('There was no main effect for time (F(4,48) = 1.869, P > 0.05)')",
  "screeningLimitations: added the modest damage stimulus ('a 12% reduction in PT ... statistical significance was not met'; prior studies 21%-44%)",
  "paper.tldr: implied swelling and strength loss were induced and not reduced -> neither arm circumference nor peak torque changed significantly over time",
  "paper.practicalImplications: dropped strength loss (not significantly induced) and limited the conclusion to this dose ('At the current dose and schedule, CBD oil may not be beneficial')"
 ],
 "residualConcerns": "Supplement study (CBD). The discussion gives the total dose as about 6 mg/kg over the protocol and about 2 mg/kg per day; the abstract reports four ANOVAs while the methods report five.",
 "spotCheckPriority": "high"
}}

