{"set": {
 "evidenceSummary": "Forty healthy, recreationally active young men (mean age 21.85 years) performed a side-cutting task with motion capture and a force plate, and OpenSim musculoskeletal models simulated a 30% increase in hip abductor force, hamstring force, both, or neither. Ligament force differed across models for the anterior ACL bundle (P = .001) and two superficial MCL bundles (P = .016 and P = .022), with the hamstring model lowest in post hoc tests, and knee adduction and internal rotation moments were also lowest in the hamstring model. Abductor-only and combined strengthening did not reduce knee ligament load.",
 "paper.findings": "The hamstring model had lower anterior ACL bundle force (F = 5.858, P = .001) and inferior superficial MCL force (P = .016) than the abductor/hamstring and abductor models, and lower posterior superficial MCL force (P = .022) than the abductor and control models. Knee adduction and internal rotation moments differed across models (P = .028 and P = .014), with the authors reporting both lowest in the hamstring model. The abductor-only and combined models did not reduce ligament load.",
 "paper.limitations": "The strength increase was simulated and not trained, the muscle force parameters come from cadaver data, only a 30% increase was modelled, and only one cutting speed was tested without fatigue or unanticipated conditions. The text says the hamstring model was lower than all other models for the knee moments, but the Table 3 footnotes mark significant pairs only for hamstring versus abductor and hamstring versus control, and the Table 2 post hoc markers do not fully match the text."
},
"verification": {
 "status": "CORRECTED",
 "changes": [
  "evidenceSummary: said only the hamstring model lowered ligament forces 'compared with the other models' -> post hoc comparisons stated per bundle; sample described as recreationally active ('participating in diverse sports activities three times a week')",
  "paper.findings: grouped the posterior MCL bundle with the abductor/hamstring and abductor comparisons -> 'lower pMCL than the abductor and control models (P < .05)'",
  "paper.limitations: added the text-versus-table conflict on post hoc pairs (Table 3 footnotes: 'Significant difference between abductor and hamstring', 'between hamstring and control') and the single 30% strengthening level"
 ],
 "residualConcerns": "Post hoc significance markers in Tables 2 and 3 are inconsistent with the results text; a reviewer may want to check the PDF tables. ACL injury-prevention framing rests on simulation only.",
 "spotCheckPriority": "high"
}}

