{"set": {
 "sports": ["Soccer", "Basketball", "Volleyball", "Other Sport", "Mixed / General Sport"],
 "evidenceSummary": "A scoping review of 16 randomised controlled trials in healthy team-sport athletes, mostly soccer, in which a cognitively demanding task (most often a 30-minute Stroop task) was compared with a control before sport-specific tests. Mental fatigue impaired intermittent endurance (Yo-Yo IR1) and technical outputs such as errors, ball losses and passing or shooting accuracy in most studies, while total distance in several small-sided games was unchanged and volleyball endurance and jump performance were unaffected. Perceived exertion rose in most studies that measured it, heart rate results were inconsistent, and blood lactate was unchanged in the one study that measured it.",
 "screeningLimitations": "Samples ranged from 9 to 48 participants, were mostly male, and were tested in laboratory or small-sided game settings, and quality appraisal (QualSyst) was used only to exclude two low-quality studies, with no grading of certainty of evidence. The abstract reports 12 included studies, while the methods, results and Table 1 report 16, and the psychophysiological section cites 19 studies, so the study count should be checked against the full text.",
